hey guys welcome back so you all know  the saying how do you get to Carnegie  Hall practice right everybody knows you  need to practice to improve on your  instrument but rarely do I hear anyone  talking about how to practice so today I  thought it'd be fun to give you some  advice that I really wish I would have  gotten when I was learning how to play  the guitar here are five common practice  mistakes  [Music]  people quit playing the guitar everyday  because they hit a wall decide they must  have no talent and give up but more  often than not their lack of progress is  due to poor and aimless practicing  habits not because you know their hands  are too small or they lack talent or  whatever other excuses we tell ourselves  so here are five common practice  mistakes that I really wish I knew when  I was learning guitar slowing down in  music is equivalent to zooming in on a  painting it allows you to see finer  detail and it's true that actually if  you play something very slow it might  actually sound worse because it  highlights and exposes your weaknesses  but if we're serious about improving on  the guitar which I hope you are then  step number one is exposing our greatest  weaknesses so that we can fix them when  I was learning electric guitar  I was so guilty of this first mistake of  playing too fast I used to just loop the  same riff or song again and again a  thousand times at the same speed and I  thought it was helping me but in fact  all it did was ingrained  bad habits into my muscle memory and  probably annoy the hell out of my  freshman year roommate I remember when I  was 17 I was obsessed with this one  technique called sweet picking it's  basically like an ascending arpeggio  done in one clean motion and I tried  that for countless hours but I always  practiced it at that exact same speed  and let's just say it didn't go well  [Music]  so if you're like I was and are tempted  to always play fast the simplest cure is  to just get a metronome it is the  easiest way to keep yourself in time  there's never been an easier time to use  one as well because there's dozens of  free apps I use one called pro metronome  on my phone and you can start by just  turning it on to something like 60 BPM  100 per second and this will force you  to play slower the other reason you need  to make the metronome your buddy is  because humans aren't born with good  rhythm it's a learned skill and so the  fingers by themselves want to rush or  slow down depending on how difficult  something is and the metronome can be  our robotic friend who kindly reminds us  where the real beat is  [Music]  so here is how I should have learned how  to sweep pick when I was 17 turn on the  metronome at something very slow 60 BPM  is always a good start each click is one  note that might seem very slow but it's  a luxury to play this slow because I  have time to think in between the notes  where's my finger going next  pinky ring finger our middle index pinky  when we're playing fast we have no time  to think about those things they just  have to be there so now that I'm playing  this slow I can focus on a few things  the first is can I actually match each  note with the metronome perfectly very  often I hear something like that when I  first show students a metronome  I was actually rushing rarely do people  go too slow they almost always rush so I  was ahead of the beats okay I'll try to  land on the other side of the beat I'll  go slower okay now I'm a little bit  behind now let's see if I can split the  difference and land rate with the  metronome okay that time I got it so  that's the type of practice that really  would have helped me learn how to sweet  pick at an early stage now of course  this has to go faster eventually so the  next thing I can do is bump up the tempo  to say 75 so we can bump it up in  increments of 15 beats per minute and we  decide to move up when it gets very easy  when we know we can do it every single  time correctly 