ate the real experience  of performing to see how the piece would  go in public and to also ensure that we  don't accidentally stop just because of  one mistake so for now on you must know  which mode you're in at all times so you  can ask yourself these questions when  you sit down  am I just trying to play through this  piece for fun then performance mode is  great you could say am i trying to see  how this would go if I was to play in a  concert tomorrow performance mode again  however if you're trying to actually  improve the song then you have to be in  practice mode to practice well we need  to set easily achievable goals that can  actually be achieved in one sitting if  we make goals too big like become a  legendary guitar player there's no way  we can accomplish that and where do we  even begin right it's too ambiguous so  ask yourself this question instead what  am i struggling with the answer could be  if you're an electric guitar player this  guitar solo from a song by Metallica or  if you're a classical guitarist you  could be Capriccio or Ave by Francisco  de aragón now we're onto something so  let's say we picked Capri to rob a by  Targa as our weak song that we want to  improve we could start from the  beginning and just keep playing until we  hit a rough patch and let's say the  rough patch happened around measure 14  like this  [Music]  problem the slur wasn't very audible the  hammer-on wasn't very audible so we  could say okay stop everything let's fix  this there's a problem now we can say  well what if I just practice the slur by  itself I think that's the issue so you  could just dribble the slur for a while  thinking about how to make a better slur  turns out if your elbow is in the pinky  is stronger once that's one thing that  might help another complication here is  that your index finger has a giant  stretch you have to reach to the seventh  fret of the fifth string and hammer on  with your pinky to the twelfth so it  helps to divide the reach between the  fingers and straighten the pinky a  little bit to make the reach easier so  those are things you could discover  while practicing very slow and once you  think it's a bit better you could plug  it back in so so far we've diagnosed the  problem the hammer on is weak we've  worked on slowly repeating it to fix it  next step is backing up a bit and giving  it a running start to see if it's fixed  [Music]  that sounded good that time if it  doesn't sound good you need to go back  to step two where you work on that bit  very slowly figuring out what's the  problem how can I fix it okay so in  short goal-oriented practicing is when  you diagnose a problem then you isolate  that moment and repeat it very slowly  there's no such thing as too slow until  that moment feels easy then you give it  a running start to try it in context and  if it goes well great you're done if it  doesn't sound good still you need to go  back to step two and work at it very  slowly  analyzing huh what's the problem here I  mean if I go slower maybe if I change  the rhythm analyze it until you think  it's easier plug it back in to see if it  works keep doing this until the problem  goes away  and lastly most guitarists lack  structure in their practice routine so I  think you should get yourself a practice  journal this is mine I think you should  go buy a very beautiful nice journal  that is designated as your music book  and nothing else so in other words don't  be writing you know your grocery list  and doodles in here this is your sacred  music book and that's all so let's talk  about how to use our practice journal  effectively open to the first blank page  and write the date in the top left  corner and when we practice we're going  to log exactly what we do and how long  we do each thing so now I'm gonna give  you my templates of how I structure my  practice routine for the most progress I  always start with a warm-up then I go on  to work on some piece that needs  improvements and then lastly I always  end by reviewing songs that I already  know that I just want to play for either  enjoyment or just to make sure that I  still have them in my fingers  [Music]  March 24th 2020 warm-up spider crawl  chromatic scale string crossing exercise  with alternation in the right hand 10  minutes total by the way if you're not  warming up before you play your songs  you're doing yourself a disservice  please do check out my video on warming  up I did a whole video talking about  exercises and the purpose of warming up  it'll revolutionize your playing so  check that out in the in the description  below  next we can jump to a song and ease  improvements let's imagine we use the  example from today Capriccio harambe  by Francisco tárrega there was a moment  in measure 14 where that Heidi hammer-on  was no good so if I were to log that  moment it would say something like  playing through Capriccio arabe measure  14 needs work I I saluted the high e  slur playing it very slow many times  then I would put an asterisk every time  I make a discovery asterisk discovered  that if my elbow is in my pinkie  hammer-ons are stronger and then lastly  you could say review of repertoire so  you could play through songs that you  already know and like for a mixture of  enjoyment and a little bit of trial and  error to see if any of the pieces need  to be touched up at all but that part  can also be just an a way to end on a  positive note and remind yourself that  we're making music after all and it's a  beautiful thing okay so there we have it  five common practice mistakes that I  really wish I knew when I was younger  and now you know them so you can try to  avoid those bad habits and by the way I  want to emphasize that I still fall into  these bad habits constantly they're hard  to avoid it takes patience and  discipline to practice well but the  reward for good practice is endless  potential for your playing so stick with  it and good luck  thanks for watching I hope you found  this useful please do subscribe if you  haven't already to see more guitar tips  in the future and we'll see you next  time  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

